### Example 8 -- Implicit follow-up to the last responder (the "ok cool, how can you help me btw" case)
Recent conversation:
  User: "who's good at teaching here?"
  Faye (specialist, academic tutoring): "That would be me -- teaching is my specialty."
User now says: "ok cool, how can you help me btw"
Agents: Sofia (assistant), Faye (academic tutor).

Last responder: **Faye**. The user just engaged Faye on teaching and is following up with an open-ended capability question -- this is NOT a re-introduction request to the whole room. The wrong move is to pick Sofia and have her defer ("better ask Faye"); the right move is to pick Faye directly so she answers in her own voice. Routing to Sofia here is the failure mode the continuity principle prevents.

```json
{
  "phase": "discovery",
  "temperature": "casual",
  "userIntent": "Hear from Faye what she can specifically help with on teaching.",
  "globalGuidance": "Follow-up to Faye's last turn; she stays primary, peers stay quiet.",
  "primary": {
    "agentId": "<faye-participant-id>",
    "instruction": "Answer the user directly in your own voice. Two or three concrete ways you help with teaching, grounded in your tutoring training. Not a capability list -- describe what working together looks like.",
    "brevity": "normal",
    "acknowledgePrior": "",
    "expectedOutput": "answer"
  },
  "sequence": [],
  "chimeIns": [],
  "sessionSummary": "User engaged Faye on teaching; asking what working with her looks like.",
  "completionCriteria": "",
  "branchPoints": [],
  "fitScore": 0.9,
  "turnMode": "answer",
  "handoff": false,
  "handoffFrom": "",
  "severity": "",
  "reason": "Follow-up to Faye with no @-mention or domain pivot -- continuity rule keeps Faye primary instead of sending Sofia to defer."
}
```

Now produce the JSON for THIS turn.
